      "questionText": "Explain the concept of 'Traceability' in agricultural export supply chains and how digital systems ensure sanitary compliance.",
      "contextScenario": "An export compliance training session for basmati rice and table grape grower cooperatives.",
      "modelAnswer": "Traceability in agricultural supply chains refers to the documented ability to identify and verify the uninterrupted history, location, agronomic inputs, and physical journey of an agricultural commodity from the primary farm parcel to the consumer's plate.\n\n1. Why Traceability is Mandatory in Export Markets:\nStringent international food safety standards (such as EU Maximum Residue Limits - MRLs, US FDA regulations, and GlobalGAP) require exporters to prove that produce is free from prohibited pesticide residues, mycotoxins, and heavy metals. When contamination occurs, targeted traceability enables rapid lot recalls without destroying uncontaminated shipments.\n\n2. Digital Traceability Mechanisms:\n- APEDA 'HortiNet' and 'GrapeNet': The Agricultural and Processed Food Products Export Development Authority (APEDA) deployed GrapeNet, an end-to-end digital tracking system for Indian export grapes.\n- Plot Registration & Input Logging: Farm plots are registered with GPS coordinates. Farmers log every pesticide spray, fertilizer application, and irrigation event via mobile apps.\n- Laboratory Assaying & QR Coding: Accredited laboratories test samples directly from registered plots and upload pesticide residue assay certificates. Packed boxes receive unique GS1 2D DataMatrix or QR codes that customs officials and European consumers can scan to view the exact farm origin, harvest date, and chemical safety clearance.",
