      "contextScenario": "Directing a student-led university technology transfer fair.",
      "modelAnswer": "Organizing an AgriTech Innovation Showcase bridges the academic laboratory with the farming community, transforming student learning into practical extension outreach.\n\n1. Pre-Event Planning & Stakeholder Engagement (Weeks 1-4):\n- Date and Venue: Schedule during a university farmer fair (Kisan Mela) at the university research farm, ensuring outdoor flight and demonstration space.\n- Farmer Mobilization: Coordinate with local KVKs, district agriculture officers, and regional FPO leaders to mobilize 200+ progressive farmers, village sarpanches, and input dealers.\n- Student Exhibitor Curation: Organize student displays across five thematic experiential zones:\n  * Zone 1: Drone Technology (Live flight demonstrations, rotary atomizers, multispectral camera payloads).\n  * Zone 2: Digital Soil Health & GIS (Live QGIS fertility map generation from farmer-submitted soil test reports).\n  * Zone 3: Mobile Apps & Extension (Hands-on tutorials for Kisan Suvidha, Meghdoot, Damini, Plantix).\n  * Zone 4: IoT Smart Farming (Low-cost LoRaWAN soil moisture sensors and automated solar drip valves).\n  * Zone 5: Biological Inputs & Bioherbicides (Live microbial culture plates, Trichoderma formulations, and bioherbicide trial pots).\n\n2. Live Demonstrations and Experiential Learning (Event Day):\n- Hands-on Smartphone Workshop: Students install Meghdoot and Damini directly onto farmers' smartphones, configuring regional language settings and GPS locations.\n- Live Flight Demonstration: A DGCA-certified student pilot executes a live 10-liter water spray flight over a demonstration crop, showing rotary atomizer droplet patterns on water-sensitive paper cards distributed to the audience.\n\n3. Feedback, Assessment, and Post-Event Documentation:\n- Interactive Farmer Feedback: Distribute bilingual feedback forms evaluating farmers' perceived usefulness, willingness to adopt, and price sensitivity.\n- Student Academic Assessment: Faculty evaluate student teams based on scientific clarity, handling farmer queries, and vernacular communication efficacy.\n- Media & Video Documentation: Produce a professional 3-minute video summary for the university website and student digital portfolios.",
